## 6. Scale envelope, truncation, recovery, concurrency

**5 GB target** `[Proposed/UNEXECUTED P1 — brief states this is a target to investigate]`: streaming-safe operations (declared-schema ingest, filter, per-group aggregate, preview, quarantine scan, hash) run row/batch-wise; the engine executes them out-of-core `[S7's reader options imply buffered, bounded reads; Inference]`. `[Executed W3-B]` demonstrates the contract-layer streaming path at toy scale (1 row resident). Global operations (full sort, distinct over all columns) may require spill or explicit memory bound: the workspace declares a spill budget; exceeding it is a clear error naming the operation, never an OOM crash. `[Choice]`

**Large-result truncation** `[Choice]`: any materialized result above N MB is written to `runs/<id>/artifacts/` and the notebook output holds a bounded inline preview + artifact reference + truncation notice. This keeps .ipynb files small (nbformat outputs are embedded in the document `[S1]`, so unbounded outputs would bloat exchange files).

**Caching & invalidation** `[Inference; mechanism S4]`: stage outputs are content-addressed in `runs/`; a stage is "clean" iff cmd + dep hashes + params + env fingerprint all match the lockfile `[S4]`. `always_changed`-style forcing exists per stage for debugging `[S4]`.

**Concurrency** `[Inference; Proposed/UNEXECUTED P6]`: one advisory lock file per project (PID + timestamp, broken only with explicit user override); datasets opened read-only by all sessions; a second opener gets read-only mode with a "who holds the lock" banner. Kernel sessions within one process are serialized per project.

**Recovery after interrupted save** `[Inference; Proposed/UNEXECUTED P4]`: all saves are write-temp + fsync + atomic rename; run events append to a journal where each event is a complete line (partial last line = interrupted, ignored, and the run marked `unverified`). A crashed save leaves the previous good file intact; a `.ipynb` that fails schema validation `[S1]` on load is opened read-only with a repair banner, never silently rewritten.

