## 7. Execution boundary for untrusted notebook code

`[Choice, constrained by brief]` Notebook code is untrusted. Clean runs execute under an OS-level sandbox per kernel process: filesystem = read allowlist (declared dataset paths + project dir) and write allowlist (`runs/<id>/` only); network disabled (loopback to the workspace's own control channel only); rlimits on CPU/wall/memory per cell timeout `[S3's timeout options are the harness's enforcement points]`. **Import blacklists are explicitly rejected** (a module needn't be importable-by-name to open a socket; the brief forbids this claim) — the boundary is the kernel's process capabilities, not its import set. Source browsing/docs reading happen in the UI process, never in the kernel, keeping "look things up" separate from "run code".

Usability trade-offs, stated honestly: (a) cells that legitimately need network or an extra file must declare it per-run (a one-click "grant network to this run" that is then recorded in the run event and **downgrades that run's replay guarantee to "partial — network granted"**); (b) sandboxing adds startup cost and occasional confusing PermissionError messages — the UI renders them as "the sandbox denied X; declare it or run interactive", not as raw tracebacks; (c) interactive sessions in the resident kernel are where analysts live, and they are *not* clean runs — the UI always labels interactive outputs "live (not a clean run)". The experiment's own executed checks ran under Bubblewrap/systemd isolation with cgroup memory caps (receipt limitations in `witnesses.json`), which is the same class of mechanism proposed here; those receipts are **not** evidence that a product kernel is isolated. `[Executed W1-3 receipts; Inference]` A sandbox-profile probe remains `[Proposed/UNEXECUTED P7 → leads.json L6]`.

